WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.870
So now that Pokemon Go has penetrated the
barrier between fiction and reality, its rich

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.590
mythology is making its way into the real
world.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.320
But some of these stories are even stranger
than fiction.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.000
The app's popularity has opened up a wellspring
of misinformation about events surrounding

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.750
the hunt for augmented reality pocket monsters.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.550
Here are a few of the more bizarre hoaxes
and why they're absolutely not true—and

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.990
why some actually are.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:26.990
Sibling stabbing

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:32.119
No, a Florida kid named Timmy did not stab
his younger brother to death after a misunderstanding

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.530
about deleted Pokemon.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.420
While the unchecked story spread like chlamydia
on social media sites, many readers failed

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.590
to realize that the fake story originated
from Cartel Press, a website which specializes

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.170
in intentionally fake news.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.329
While many of their headlines are obviously
satirical, such as "Zoo claims gorilla had

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:52.239
ties to ISIS," the story of young, murderous
Timmy offered no humor in its retelling.

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:55.800
Even if you ignore the completely fake nature
of the site, there are absolutely no incidents

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.870
reported in Jacksonville similar to this event.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.579
Additionally, you don't even really delete
your Pokemon; you just trade them in for delicious

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:02.579
candies.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:03.579
Get with it, noob.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:05.830
You'll begin to notice that Cartel Press is
a trend here.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:10.500
"You will never find a more wretched hive
of scum and villainy."

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.600
The $500 million lawsuit

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.710
Another story that traces back to Cartel Press
is the tale of Charley, who wandered into

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.180
the street while playing Pokemon Go, was hit
by a car, and is now suing app makers Niantic

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.829
for $500 million.

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:28.850
While the post's language is much more obviously
a parody of the frivolous litigation trend,

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.640
readers were once again fooled.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.909
To date, Niantic has not been the subject
of any Pokemon Go-related injury lawsuits.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:39.650
However, Niantic’s previous GPS-based game,
Ingress, is related to at least two reported

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:40.650
deaths.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.390
A gamer in Dublin fell off a pier in 2016
while playing the game.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.549
Another player wandered into traffic and was
hit by a bus in Rio de Janeiro in 2014.

00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:52.290
Vice interviewed Cartel Press owner Pablo
Reyes, whose only reasoning for spreading

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.469
paranoia was this:

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:56.630
"People always expect something anyways so
if you write about it beforehand it's like

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:57.630
you predicted it."

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.950
Psychic-type Pokemon, look out.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.240
You've got some competition.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.049
Bad Neighborhood

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:07.000
Because the bogus news from the Onion-wannabe
never ends, we can also confirm that "Marcus

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.630
Jackson" did not get stabbed during his Pokemon
adventures because he, and we quote,

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.670
"...didn't realize he had stepped into what
is know [sic] as a bad neighborhood."

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.760
Also from Cartel Press, and therefore inherently
false: the rumor of the highway Pikachu hundred-car

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.900
pileup, and ISIS taking credit for Pokemon
Go's login issues.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.670
True, however, is the remarkably similar story
of Michael Baker, who was stabbed while playing

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:29.840
in Forest Grove, Oregon.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.250
The player initially refused treatment for
his injuries so that he could continue playing,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.540
but later sought medical attention, according
to an interview with KATU 2 News.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.670
"I'm just gonna keep catchin' 'em all ya know?

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.080
That's, that's pretty much what's gonna happen."

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.090
No Trespassing

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.530
Another fake news site reported that a kid
named "Arthur Disby" was shot when he entered

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.840
the house of an aging widow in order to catch
a wild Pokemon.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.620
While The National Report is another veiled
satire site, many readers believed that the

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.060
story was completely real, based on comments
left on the page.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.170
There have been no confirmed reports of breaking
and entering in order to catch imaginary monsters,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.470
primarily due to the fact that you simply
don't need to be that close to a Pokemon to

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.090
be able to capture it.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.770
Based on the game's geolocation, you can usually
catch any Pokemon that happens to pop up within

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.730
the borders of a building without actually
entering it.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.250
And now you know.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.400
Simpsons did it

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.160
While The Simpsons has predicted plenty of
things, internet hoaxes often give the show

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.190
more credit than it deserves.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.690
Despite popular opinion, the show did not
predict Donald Trump's campaign announcement,

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.390
nor did it anticipate the creation of Pokemon
Go.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.120
In a currently popular meme, a four-fingered,
yellow hand points at an empty corner while

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.420
a smartphone in the other hand displays a
Pikachu.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.760
Efforts have been made to make this truly
look like an older screencap from the show,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.880
but it's all Photoshop, folks.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.390
You can see the original clip in the episode
"How I Spent My Strummer Vacation."

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.840
"But he's just like you or me or Jesus over
there!"

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.750
The inevitable fee

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:53.440
If you want to send someone into a panic,
tell them that they have to pay for a once-free

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:54.440
service.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.590
Like the hundreds of "Facebook fee" scares
that preceded them, the Pokemon Go fee rumors

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.950
are false.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.360
Seeing an opportunity to ride the wave of
the game, scammers began sending out emails

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.020
claiming that Pokemon Go would soon cost $12.99
per month to play, mostly so Niantic could

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.650
beef up their straining servers.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.880
And apparently augment the fortune already
being spent in the app's shop.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.790
Niantic has not announced a fee, nor do they
have any plans to, which has been confirmed

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:17.790
by The Verge.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.040
Scammers, however, have been using these fake
announcements to collect users' personal information.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.880
Don't fall for it.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.160
Land Down Under

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:29.600
And no, Australian police are not targeting
Pokemon Go players, despite a popular image

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.900
that's been circulating online.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.730
While the image does come from a real sign
in Australia, it's also been the subject of

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.980
countless manipulations over the years.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.620
There's no reason for police officers to target
users of an app about imaginary animal poaching.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.500
They almost definitely have bigger problems
to worry about.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:45.580
"That's not a knife.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.200
That's a knife."

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.410
The Murder Witness

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:56.720
According to initial accounts, an Uber driver
named Alex Ramirez was streaming his Pokemon

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.770
Go adventures between stops and witnessed
a body being tossed out of a car.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:10.520
"Oh my god. Oh my god! Holy s***! Oh god!
This guy just killed this chick, oh f***!

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.600
Holy s***! This guy just killed, oh my god,
oh my god I just witnessed a f****** murder!"

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.340
He subsequently called 911, gave the location
of the body, and reported being followed by

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.800
the murderers…all before getting fired by
Uber. The video was convincing enough that

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.940
a few top YouTube hoax debunkers fell for
it, but none actually bothered to verify their

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:27.600
sources.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.120
Gizmodo reports that calls to the local police
revealed that there were no bodies found,

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.440
nor any murders matching that description.
Calls to Uber also revealed that they hadn't

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.220
fired anyone over a Pokemon incident, only
temporarily suspended a driver after rider

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:44.020
complaints. This was because Ramirez was playing
while driving at high speeds, which is obvious

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.760
on his channel. GoFundMe quickly pulled the
page set up to help Ramirez, since it was

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.680
put up under false pretenses, and the whole
thing was revealed to be a huge publicity

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.400
stunt. And possibly a serious case of fraud.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:58.640
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.160
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which Pokemon Go hoaxes you fell for…

